in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @mirror/store
in @mirror/au

# @mirror/shatter — the optional disk projection of au + splinter + mosaic.
#
# Recognition (docs/shatter-spec.md, 2026-06-04 reframe; sharpened
# 2026-06-06 by Alex's three-layer recognition at @glass): the
# fragmentation store IS canonical. `.shatter` is ONE projection
# format — a settled .mirror file that knows what it cost. Five
# sections, all native mirror syntax, all content-addressed, all
# feedable back into the compiler. The projection round-trips: parse
# a .shatter, re-emit, get the same OID. That fixed point IS what
# makes .shatter a crystal and not a dump.
#
# Header "au + splinter + mosaic" is substrate-honest under the
# three-layer recognition: au is the proposed composition of
# splinters (the Fate-emitted form); splinter is the atomic
# content-addressed unit (per @glass); mosaic is the algebra that
# composes splinters into au and settles au into shard. The
# .shatter file projects the composition's fragment_tree (the
# splinter set organized as the @store splinter_graph closure),
# the transparency, the properties, the kernel, and the fate.
#
# Recognition (docs/specs/au-and-conductivity.md, C5): au's identity
# IS the content-hash of (altitude, content, transparency). The
# .shatter OID is derived from those same three coordinates lifted
# through the five-section structure; the projection preserves au's
# identity contract because the sections ARE the three coordinates
# unrolled (fragment_tree carries content, kernel/fate parametrize
# altitude, transparency IS transparency, properties witness the
# verification at that altitude). Two au values with equal identity
# project to byte-equal .shatter files.
#
# Idempotence contract: shatter(a) and shatter(shatter(a) -> au) settle
# to the same oid. Per the spec's fixed-point clause:
#     mirror compile source.mirror -> output.shatter
#     mirror compile output.shatter -> output.shatter
# The recompilation is a no-op at the content-address layer because
# the inputs to the hash (altitude, content, transparency) are
# preserved across project/ingest.
#
# Note: the store is canonical. .shatter is ONE projection. Other
# projections (binary archives, content-only dumps, language-specific
# embeddings) live elsewhere; the store does not require any
# particular on-disk format. Per shards/mirror/store.mirror's framing.
#
# Reconciliation with au.mirror (C5): au.mirror declared the surface
#     shatter(a: au) -> ref { \ }
# at au's altitude — au knows how to project itself. This shard
# realizes that surface as `project(a: au) -> shatter` (the canonical
# direction) plus `ingest(s: shatter) -> au` (the parse-back direction
# that closes the fixed-point loop). au's stub does not need
# updating; this shard IS its body at the disk-projection altitude.
#
# Reconciliation with mosaic.mirror (C2): mosaic declared
#     shatter(au, transparency) -> ref { \ }
# at the build altitude — the build-time trigger that emits .shatter
# alongside the source when `mirror kintsugi --emit shatter` runs
# (mosaic.md §10.6, explicit-emit policy). mosaic's shatter is the
# build-altitude porcelain; this shard's `project` is the substrate
# the porcelain calls into. au.mirror's `shatter` is canonical; the
# two appearances at higher altitudes both delegate here.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/shatter-spec.md (the five sections,
# idempotent fixed-point, content-addressing, autopoietic loop),
# docs/specs/mosaic.md §7 (the build-time emit trigger),
# docs/specs/au-and-conductivity.md (the identity contract that
# guarantees idempotence).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-au-conductivity]] (au IS the
#                      Fate output type; .shatter projects au).
# Related shards: au.mirror (the surface declaration this realizes),
#                 store.mirror (oid + splinter_graph — the content-
#                 address floor), mosaic.mirror (the build-altitude
#                 trigger), glass.mirror (splinter / shard /
#                 transparency / property verdict carriers).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/shatter.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/shatter` and only that.

# === the five-section structure on disk ===
#
# Per docs/specs/shatter-spec.md (post-MirrorLoss -> transparency
# migration). Each field IS a projection of substrate state already
# held in the fragmentation store; the on-disk form makes it
# portable. Order is canonical: fragment_tree first because it's
# the content; transparency second because it's the trace;
# properties third because they witness the trace; kernel fourth
# because it parametrizes dispatch; fate fifth because it carries
# the model that produced the rest.
#
# Section 2 IS `transparency(au)` — the typed verdict carrier from
# prism/imperfect/src/transparency.rs (per glass.mirror's
# transparency<p> floor). MirrorLoss is DEAD; the Transparency<Ref>
# algebra (Fail-dominates / Partial-min-confidence / Pass-neutral)
# composes phase verdicts across the build.
#
# `fragment_tree: splinter_graph` — the OID-graph projection of the
# splinter set (per @mirror/store's splinter_graph: root + children
# in dependency-closure order). Each splinter referenced by oid is
# content-addressed at its own altitude; the splinter_graph carries
# the wiring. Under the 2026-06-06 vocabulary alignment, this field
# is the graph-projection carrier (not the atomic-unit carrier; the
# atom is `splinter` at @glass).
type shatter = {
  fragment_tree: splinter_graph,    # the AST closure as OID-graph (section 1)
  transparency: transparency(au),   # located opacity (section 2, was MirrorLoss)
  properties: [property_verdict],   # what verified (section 3)
  kernel: kernel_spec,              # decomposition params (section 4)
  fate: fate_weights,               # the model that compiled it (section 5)
}

# === helper types: opaque carriers from neighboring altitudes ===
#
# These are forward references; the concrete definitions live where
# the substrate already declares them. `kernel_spec` lives in
# @epistemologic/silicon (the spectral decomposition altitude);
# `fate_weights` lives in @fate (the 450-parameter selector that
# carries the five models). This shard treats them as opaque refs
# so the projection compiles without pulling those altitudes in.
type kernel_spec = ref
type fate_weights = ref

# === mirror shatter plumbing — direct content-store access ===
#
# Analogous to `git cat-file` / `git hash-object`. `project` writes
# the canonical .shatter form from an au; `ingest` parses .shatter
# back into an au by re-hashing through the store. The pair
# realizes the spec's fixed-point clause: project . ingest = id at
# the oid level, ingest . project = id at the au level (modulo
# transparency-tracked opacities).

# project(a) -> shatter
#   au -> .shatter projection (canonical). au.mirror's `shatter(a)
#   -> ref` stub resolves through this action — the substrate
#   reads au's three identity coordinates (altitude, content,
#   transparency) and unrolls them into the five sections. The
#   output's oid equals au's identity oid; same au, same .shatter,
#   byte-equal.
project(a: au) -> shatter { \ }

# ingest(s) -> au
#   .shatter -> store (parse back). Re-enters the substrate by
#   re-hashing the five sections under the store's content
#   algebra (CoincidenceHash<5,5> per shards/prism.mirror). The
#   returned au has the same identity oid the .shatter was
#   projected from, closing the autopoietic loop (spec §"The
#   Autopoietic Loop").
ingest(s: shatter) -> au { \ }

out @mirror/shatter
out shatter
out project
out ingest
